            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus primarily understands the helmet of salvation as the believer's assurance or hope of salvation, which protects the mind and thoughts from spiritual attack. Recognized alternatives include the divine warrior motif (it is God's own armor given to believers) and a christological reading where the helmet represents Christ Himself.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book identifies the helmet objectively as Christ (the Head) and as God's own armor lent to His people, specifically functioning to protect the head as the spiritual locus where true or counterfeit allegiance is marked.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Subjective assurance/hope of salvation protecting the mind, or objectively God's own divine armor / Christ Himself.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Christ as the Head and God's lent armor, protecting the mark of allegiance.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"While drawing on recognized minority/alternative traditions (Christ as the helmet, God's lent armor), the book shifts the functional focus of the helmet from protecting the believer's internal psychological state (mind/thoughts) to protecting the external/spiritual locus of allegiance (the forehead/head).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book selects valid objective threads from the consensus and refines the anatomical symbolism (mind vs. locus of allegiance) without flatly contradicting the broader metaphor of spiritual protection.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's core aligns with recognized alternative traditions in the consensus (Isaiah 59's divine armor, union with Christ). Its novelty lies in classifying the 'head' being protected not as the modern cognitive 'mind', but as the biblical site of allegiance (foreheads marked for God or the beast), meriting a REFINED classification.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The primary consensus heavily relies on 1 Thessalonians 5:8 to frame the helmet as the subjective 'hope' of salvation protecting the intellect/emotions. By focusing objectively on Christ and allegiance, the book bypasses the most common pastoral application of mental assurance.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
